(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-11-04"] = {"chem-11-04-b01":{"idea":"Oxidation and reduction are described by electron movement. Remember OIL RIG: Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain.","steps":["When an atom loses electrons, its oxidation number goes up.","Losing electrons is oxidation.","Example: Na → Na⁺ + e⁻."],"others":"Gain of electrons is reduction, the opposite process. Protons are not moved in redox, and losing or gaining protons describes acid-base ideas, not oxidation.","trap":"OIL RIG.","answer":"Loss of electrons"},"chem-11-04-b02":{"idea":"A reducing agent helps another species gain electrons. It does this by giving up its own electrons.","steps":["The reducing agent loses electrons, so it is oxidised.","The electrons go to the other species, which is reduced.","So it is oxidised itself and causes reduction of the other."],"others":"A species that is reduced itself and oxidises the other is the oxidising agent. Accepting only protons is acid-base behaviour. A substance that stays unchanged is not part of the redox change at all.","trap":"The reducing agent is the one that gets oxidised.","answer":"Gets oxidized itself, and causes reduction of the other species"},"chem-11-04-b03":{"idea":"In an ion or molecule, the oxidation numbers of all atoms add up to the total charge.","steps":["Let the oxidation number of S be x.","In SO₄²⁻: x + 4(−2) = −2.","x − 8 = −2. x = +6."],"trap":"Oxygen is −2, and there are four of them. Do not forget to multiply.","answer":"+6"},"chem-11-04-b04":{"idea":"An atom in its free element form has not gained or lost electrons to any other atom.","steps":["Cl₂ and Fe(s) contain atoms of only one element.","Electrons are shared equally or not exchanged at all.","So the oxidation number is zero."],"others":"+1 and −1 are charges seen in compounds. Group number is not an oxidation number rule. Free elements always have zero.","trap":"Free element: always 0.","answer":"Zero"},"chem-11-04-b05":{"idea":"To find what is oxidised, see which species loses electrons and its oxidation number rises.","steps":["Zn goes from 0 to +2 in ZnSO₄. It loses 2 electrons.","Cu²⁺ goes from +2 to 0. It gains 2 electrons.","Loss of electrons is oxidation, so Zn is oxidised."],"others":"Cu goes from +2 to 0, so it is reduced. Oxygen and sulfur in sulfate keep the same oxidation numbers, so they are not oxidised.","trap":"Oxidation number going up means oxidised.","answer":"Zn (0 -> +2)"},"chem-11-04-b06":{"idea":"Hydrogen has oxidation number +1 when it is bonded to non-metals.","steps":["In H₂O, O is −2, so two H must total +2. Each H is +1.","In HCl, Cl is −1, so H is +1.","So H is +1 in most compounds."],"others":"−1 occurs only in metal hydrides like NaH or CaH₂. +2 is never seen for hydrogen. 0 is for free H₂.","trap":"Exception: hydrides of metals give −1.","answer":"+1"},"chem-11-04-b07":{"idea":"Oxygen is very electronegative, so it usually takes electrons.","steps":["In CO₂ and H₂O, oxygen pulls electrons from the other atoms.","Each oxygen atom gains 2 electrons.","So its oxidation number is −2."],"others":"−1 is for peroxides like H₂O₂. +2 appears only in OF₂. 0 is for free O₂.","trap":"Exceptions: peroxides (−1) and OF₂ (+2).","answer":"-2"},"chem-11-04-b08":{"idea":"Redox reactions are grouped by pattern. Two or more substances joining to give one product is combination.","steps":["2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO has two reactants and one product.","Mg goes 0 → +2 (oxidised). O goes 0 → −2 (reduced).","So it is a combination reaction."],"others":"Displacement needs one element to push another out of a compound. Decomposition needs one compound to split. Disproportionation needs the same element to be both oxidised and reduced.","trap":"Many → one means combination.","answer":"Combination reaction"},"chem-11-04-b09":{"idea":"A decomposition reaction is one in which a single compound breaks into simpler substances.","steps":["KClO₃ is the only reactant.","It breaks into KCl and O₂.","Cl goes +5 → −1 (reduced) and O goes −2 → 0 (oxidised).","So it is a decomposition redox reaction."],"others":"Combination would need several reactants. Displacement needs an element and a compound as reactants. Neutralisation needs an acid and a base, and has no change in oxidation number.","trap":"One reactant, many products: decomposition.","answer":"Decomposition reaction"},"chem-11-04-b10":{"idea":"An oxidising agent takes electrons from another species. This makes the other species oxidised.","steps":["The oxidising agent gains electrons.","So its oxidation number falls and it is reduced.","The species that lost the electrons is oxidised."],"others":"A species that is oxidised and causes reduction is the reducing agent. Donating only protons is acid behaviour. A substance that stays unchanged is not part of the redox change.","trap":"The oxidising agent is itself reduced.","answer":"Gets reduced itself, and causes oxidation of the other species"},"chem-11-04-b11":{"idea":"In an ion or molecule, the oxidation numbers of all atoms add up to the total charge.","steps":["Let the oxidation number of N be x.","In NO₃⁻: x + 3(−2) = −1.","x − 6 = −1. x = +5."],"trap":"Total charge is −1, not zero.","answer":"+5"},"chem-11-04-b12":{"idea":"A free element has oxidation number zero.","steps":["Cl₂ has two identical atoms.","They share electrons equally, so neither gains nor loses any.","So the oxidation number is 0."],"others":"−1 is chlorine in chlorides like NaCl. +1 is chlorine in hypochlorites. +7 is chlorine in perchlorates. None of these apply to Cl₂ alone.","trap":"Same atoms bonded together: 0.","answer":"0"},"chem-11-04-b13":{"idea":"Find the oxidation number of each element before and after the reaction.","steps":["Na goes from 0 to +1. It loses an electron, so it is oxidised.","Cl goes from 0 to −1. It gains an electron, so it is reduced.","So Na is oxidised and Cl is reduced."],"others":"The opposite assignment mixes up loss and gain. Both elements do change oxidation number. Both cannot be oxidised, since electrons lost by Na must go to Cl.","trap":"In every redox, one species is oxidised and one is reduced.","answer":"Na is oxidized (0 -> +1) and Cl is reduced (0 -> -1)"},"chem-11-04-b14":{"idea":"Alkali metals have one outer electron, which they lose easily.","steps":["Group 1 atoms have 1 valence electron.","They lose it to form M⁺.","So the oxidation number is always +1 in compounds."],"others":"+2 is for Group 2 metals. −1 would need them to gain an electron, which they do not do. They do not have a variable range.","trap":"Group 1: +1. Group 2: +2.","answer":"Always +1"},"chem-11-04-b15":{"idea":"A redox reaction is a reaction with electron transfer. Oxidation and reduction always come together.","steps":["One species loses electrons and is oxidised.","Another gains those electrons and is reduced.","Both happen at the same time."],"others":"Releasing heat does not make a reaction redox. An acid-base reaction has no change in oxidation numbers. A reaction with no change in any oxidation number is not redox.","trap":"Redox: two changes in one reaction.","answer":"A reaction in which oxidation and reduction occur simultaneously"},"chem-11-04-b16":{"idea":"In an ion or molecule, the oxidation numbers of all atoms add up to the total charge.","steps":["Let the oxidation number of Cr be x.","In Cr₂O₇²⁻: 2x + 7(−2) = −2.","2x − 14 = −2, so 2x = 12. x = +6."],"trap":"There are 2 Cr atoms, so divide by 2 at the end.","answer":"+6"},"chem-11-04-b17":{"idea":"In an ion or molecule, the oxidation numbers of all atoms add up to the total charge.","steps":["Let the oxidation number of Mn be x.","In MnO₄⁻: x + 4(−2) = −1.","x − 8 = −1. x = +7."],"trap":"Mn in permanganate is at its highest oxidation state.","answer":"+7"},"chem-11-04-b18":{"idea":"In an ion or molecule, the oxidation numbers of all atoms add up to the total charge.","steps":["Let the oxidation number of Mn be x.","In MnO₄²⁻: x + 4(−2) = −2.","x − 8 = −2. x = +6."],"others":"+7 is for permanganate, whose charge is −1. +2 and +4 do not fit the equation x − 8 = −2.","trap":"Charge −2 here, not −1. That changes +7 to +6.","answer":"+6"},"chem-11-04-b19":{"idea":"In an ion or molecule, the oxidation numbers of all atoms add up to the total charge.","steps":["Let the oxidation number of S be x.","In SO₃²⁻: x + 3(−2) = −2.","x − 6 = −2. x = +4."],"others":"+6 is sulfur in sulfate (SO₄²⁻), which has one more oxygen. +2 and −2 do not satisfy x − 6 = −2.","trap":"Sulfite has three O, sulfate has four O.","answer":"+4"},"chem-11-04-b20":{"idea":"When two atoms of an element are in different surroundings, the answer is an average. Use the same sum rule for the whole ion.","steps":["Let the average oxidation number of S be x.","In S₂O₃²⁻: 2x + 3(−2) = −2.","2x − 6 = −2, so 2x = 4.","x = +2 (average)."],"others":"+6 is sulfur in sulfate. +4 is sulfur in sulfite. 0 would need the oxygen charges to cancel, which they do not here.","trap":"Divide by 2 because there are two S atoms.","answer":"+2"},"chem-11-04-b21":{"idea":"Disproportionation is when one element in one oxidation state goes up and down at the same time.","steps":["Cl in Cl₂ is 0.","In NaCl, Cl is −1, so it is reduced.","In NaOCl, Cl is +1, so it is oxidised.","The same element is both oxidised and reduced: disproportionation."],"others":"Displacement would keep oxidation numbers unchanged here, which is false. Only oxidation is wrong because NaCl also forms. Only reduction is wrong because NaOCl also forms.","trap":"One element, two products at higher and lower states.","answer":"Disproportionation: Cl goes from 0 to +1 (NaOCl) and to -1 (NaCl)"},"chem-11-04-b22":{"idea":"Look at the oxygen in H₂O₂ and see where it ends up.","steps":["In H₂O₂, each O is −1 (peroxide).","In H₂O, O is −2. This is reduction.","In O₂, O is 0. This is oxidation.","The same element goes both up and down: disproportionation."],"others":"Only oxidation from −2 is wrong, because O in H₂O₂ is −1, not −2. Only reduction is wrong, because O₂ is formed. No redox is wrong, as oxidation numbers change.","trap":"Peroxide oxygen is −1, between −2 and 0.","answer":"Disproportionation: O goes from -1 to -2 (H2O) and to 0 (O2)"},"chem-11-04-b23":{"idea":"In a compound, the sum of oxidation numbers is zero. Use the known value of hydrogen.","steps":["Let N be x. Three H atoms are +1 each.","x + 3(+1) = 0. x = −3."],"others":"+3 and +1 would be right for a more oxidised N bonded to O. −1 does not satisfy x + 3 = 0.","trap":"N is more electronegative than H, so N is negative.","answer":"-3"},"chem-11-04-b24":{"idea":"Set the sum of the oxidation numbers in N₂H₄ to zero.","steps":["Let N be x. There are 2 N and 4 H.","2x + 4(+1) = 0.","2x = −4. x = −2."],"others":"−3 is for NH₃. −1 and +2 do not satisfy 2x + 4 = 0.","trap":"Two N atoms share the total, so divide by 2.","answer":"-2"},"chem-11-04-b25":{"idea":"Use the sum rule: H is +1 and O is −2 in the neutral acid HClO₄.","steps":["Let Cl be x.","(+1) + x + 4(−2) = 0.","x − 7 = 0. x = +7."],"others":"+5 is chlorine in chlorate. +3 is chlorine in chlorite. +1 is chlorine in hypochlorite. Perchlorate has four oxygen atoms and the highest value.","trap":"More O means higher oxidation number of Cl.","answer":"+7"},"chem-11-04-b26":{"idea":"Use the sum rule: H is +1 and O is −2 in the neutral acid HClO₃.","steps":["Let Cl be x.","(+1) + x + 3(−2) = 0.","x − 5 = 0. x = +5."],"others":"+7 needs four O atoms. +3 needs two O atoms. +1 needs one O atom. Here there are three O atoms.","trap":"Count the O atoms.","answer":"+5"},"chem-11-04-b27":{"idea":"Use the sum rule: H is +1 and O is −2 in the neutral acid HClO.","steps":["Let Cl be x.","(+1) + x + (−2) = 0.","x − 1 = 0. x = +1."],"others":"+3 needs two O atoms. +5 needs three O atoms. −1 would be for chloride, which has no oxygen.","trap":"One O gives +1 for Cl.","answer":"+1"},"chem-11-04-b28":{"idea":"The average oxidation number of an element in a compound comes from the sum rule over all atoms.","steps":["Let the average oxidation number of C be x.","6x + 12(+1) + 6(−2) = 0.","6x + 12 − 12 = 0. x = 0."],"others":"−2, +2 and +4 would need the carbon total to cancel something other than zero. Here the H and O contributions cancel exactly.","trap":"This is an average. Different C atoms in glucose have different values.","answer":"0"},"chem-11-04-b29":{"idea":"A balanced redox equation must balance both atoms and charge. Electrons lost must equal electrons gained.","steps":["Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ loses 1 electron.","Cl₂ → 2Cl⁻ gains 2 electrons.","So 2Fe²⁺ are needed for each Cl₂.","2Fe²⁺ + Cl₂ → 2Fe³⁺ + 2Cl⁻. Charge: +4 − 0 = +6 − 2. Both sides are +4."],"others":"Fe²⁺ + Cl₂ gives charge +2 on the left and +1 on the right, so it is not balanced. Fe²⁺ + 2Cl₂ with 4Cl⁻ is not balanced for electrons. The last equation has Cl₂ on both sides and no reduction.","trap":"Check both atoms and total charge.","answer":"2Fe2+ + Cl2 -> 2Fe3+ + 2Cl-"},"chem-11-04-b30":{"idea":"In a displacement reaction, a more reactive element pushes a less reactive element out of its compound.","steps":["Fe is more reactive than Cu.","Fe goes 0 → +2. Cu²⁺ goes +2 → 0.","Fe takes the place of Cu in the compound: displacement."],"others":"CaCO₃ splitting is decomposition. H₂ + Cl₂ is combination. NaOH + HCl is neutralisation, with no change in oxidation number.","trap":"Element + compound → new element + new compound.","answer":"Fe + CuSO4 -> FeSO4 + Cu"},"chem-11-04-b31":{"idea":"In an ion or molecule, the oxidation numbers of all atoms add up to the total charge.","steps":["Let N be x.","In NO₂⁻: x + 2(−2) = −1.","x − 4 = −1. x = +3."],"others":"+5 is for nitrate, which has three oxygen atoms. −3 is for ammonia. +1 does not satisfy x − 4 = −1.","trap":"Nitrite (2 O) is +3. Nitrate (3 O) is +5.","answer":"+3"},"chem-11-04-b32":{"idea":"Use the balanced equation as a mole ratio. Each Cl₂ takes 2 electrons.","steps":["2Fe²⁺ + Cl₂ → 2Fe³⁺ + 2Cl⁻.","Ratio Fe²⁺ : Cl₂ = 2 : 1.","Moles of Cl₂ = 1 × (1/2).","= 0.5 mol."],"trap":"Check the electrons: 1 mol Fe²⁺ gives 1 mol e⁻. One Cl₂ takes 2 e⁻, so 0.5 mol Cl₂ is enough.","answer":"0.5 mol"},"chem-11-04-b33":{"idea":"In an ion or molecule, the oxidation numbers of all atoms add up to the total charge.","steps":["Let I be x.","In IO₃⁻: x + 3(−2) = −1.","x − 6 = −1. x = +5."],"others":"−1 is iodide. +7 would need four oxygen atoms (periodate). +3 does not satisfy x − 6 = −1.","trap":"Charge is −1, three O.","answer":"+5"},"chem-11-04-b34":{"idea":"Balance the atoms and the charges. The electrons lost by Cu must equal the electrons gained by Ag⁺.","steps":["Cu → Cu²⁺ loses 2 electrons.","Each Ag⁺ → Ag gains 1 electron.","So 2 Ag⁺ are needed for 1 Cu.","Cu + 2Ag⁺ → Cu²⁺ + 2Ag. Charge is +2 on each side."],"others":"Cu + Ag⁺ with Cu²⁺ + Ag has charge +1 on the left and +2 on the right. 2Cu + Ag⁺ has the ratio inverted. Ag₂ is not how silver metal is written, and it also cannot fix the charge balance.","trap":"Always check the charge on both sides.","answer":"Cu + 2Ag+ -> Cu2+ + 2Ag"},"chem-11-04-b35":{"idea":"The n-factor is the number of electrons gained by one formula unit. It equals the fall in oxidation number times atoms of that element.","steps":["In acid, MnO₄⁻ is reduced to Mn²⁺.","Mn goes from +7 to +2, a fall of 5.","Only one Mn atom is present.","n-factor = 5."],"others":"7 is the starting oxidation number, not the change. 2 is the final oxidation number. 1 would hold only for reduction in different conditions to a different product.","trap":"Count the change, not the starting value.","answer":"5"},"chem-11-04-b36":{"idea":"The n-factor depends on how much each atom changes and how many atoms there are.","steps":["In acid, Cr₂O₇²⁻ is reduced to Cr³⁺.","Cr goes from +6 to +3, a fall of 3 per atom.","There are 2 Cr atoms.","n-factor = 3 × 2 = 6."],"others":"3 forgets that there are 2 Cr atoms. 2 is the number of Cr atoms only. 12 doubles the right value by counting the change twice.","trap":"Multiply the change by the number of atoms.","answer":"6"},"chem-11-04-b37":{"idea":"In metal hydrides, hydrogen takes an electron from the metal. Hydrogen is then negative.","steps":["Ca is a metal, with oxidation number +2.","CaH₂ is neutral: (+2) + 2x = 0. x = −1.","So H is −1."],"others":"+1 is the usual value in non-metal compounds. 0 is for free H₂. −2 would not balance +2 over two H atoms.","trap":"Hydrides of metals: H = −1.","answer":"-1"},"chem-11-04-b38":{"idea":"A combination reaction forms one product from two or more reactants. It is redox when oxidation numbers change.","steps":["N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃ has two reactants and one product.","N goes 0 → −3 and H goes 0 → +1.","Oxidation numbers change, so it is a combination redox reaction."],"others":"AgNO₃ + NaCl is a precipitation, with no oxidation number change. 2H₂O splitting is decomposition. CaCO₃ splitting is also decomposition and has no change in oxidation numbers.","trap":"Two reactants, one product, with changes in oxidation number.","answer":"N2 + 3H2 -> 2NH3"},"chem-11-04-b39":{"idea":"A redox reaction needs a change in oxidation number. Precipitation reactions swap ions only.","steps":["Ag is +1 before and after in AgCl.","Cl is −1 before and after.","Na is +1 and N is +5 in both nitrates.","No oxidation number changes, so it is a double displacement, not redox."],"others":"Ag⁺ is not reduced to Ag metal. N in nitrate stays +5. Na does not displace Ag, as both stay as ions.","trap":"Swapping partners is not redox.","answer":"No element changes oxidation number; it is a double displacement"},"chem-11-04-b40":{"idea":"Na₂O₂ is a peroxide. Oxygen atoms are bonded to each other, which changes their oxidation number.","steps":["Na is +1. Two Na give +2.","The compound is neutral: 2(+1) + 2x = 0.","2x = −2. x = −1."],"trap":"In a peroxide, the O–O bond means oxygen is −1, not −2.","answer":"-1"},"chem-11-04-b41":{"idea":"Fe₃O₄ gives an average oxidation number because it contains Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺ together.","steps":["Let the average oxidation number of Fe be x.","3x + 4(−2) = 0. 3x = 8.","x = +8/3, about +2.67."],"others":"+2 is only the FeO part. +3 is only the Fe₂O₃ part. +4 wrongly gives 8 to two atoms, but there are three Fe atoms.","trap":"Actually Fe₃O₄ = FeO·Fe₂O₃, so 1 Fe²⁺ and 2 Fe³⁺: average 8/3.","answer":"+8/3 (about +2.67)"},"chem-11-04-b42":{"idea":"Fluorine is the most electronegative element. It is always −1, which forces oxygen to be positive here.","steps":["F is −1. There are 2 F atoms, giving −2.","OF₂ is neutral: x + 2(−1) = 0. x = +2."],"others":"−2 is oxygen's usual value but F is more electronegative. −1 is for peroxides. 0 is for free O₂.","trap":"Only compound where O is positive.","answer":"+2"},"chem-11-04-b43":{"idea":"Balance redox equations by making the electrons lost equal to the electrons gained. Then balance O with H₂O and H with H⁺.","steps":["MnO₄⁻ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ (gains 5 electrons).","Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻ (loses 1 electron), so use 5 Fe²⁺.","Oxygen: 4 O on the left gives 4 H₂O on the right.","4 H₂O needs 8 H⁺ on the left.","MnO₄⁻ + 5Fe²⁺ + 8H⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 5Fe³⁺ + 4H₂O. The coefficient of H⁺ is 8."],"trap":"Check charge: left −1 + 10 + 8 = +17. Right +2 + 15 = +17.","answer":"8"},"chem-11-04-b44":{"idea":"Balance the electrons first. Then fix oxygen with H₂O and hydrogen with H⁺.","steps":["Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ (gains 6 electrons).","Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻ (loses 1 electron), so 6 Fe²⁺ are needed.","7 O on the left gives 7 H₂O, which need 14 H⁺.","Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 6Fe²⁺ + 14H⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + 6Fe³⁺ + 7H₂O. The coefficient of Fe²⁺ is 6."],"trap":"Check charge: left −2 + 12 + 14 = +24. Right 6 + 18 = +24.","answer":"6"},"chem-11-04-b45":{"idea":"Disproportionation is when the same element is both oxidised and reduced. Look at where each Cl goes.","steps":["Cl in Cl₂ is 0.","In NaCl, Cl is −1. Five Cl atoms gain 1 electron each: 5 electrons.","In NaClO₃, Cl is +5. One Cl atom loses 5 electrons.","Electrons lost = electrons gained, so it is disproportionation."],"others":"Comproportionation is the reverse: two states making one. Only oxidation is wrong, since NaCl contains Cl at −1. Only reduction is wrong, since NaClO₃ has Cl at +5, and its oxygen comes from NaOH, but that is not the point.","trap":"Hot and concentrated alkali gives chlorate. Cold and dilute gives hypochlorite.","answer":"Disproportionation: 5 Cl go from 0 to -1 (NaCl), 1 Cl goes from 0 to +5 (NaClO3)"},"chem-11-04-b46":{"idea":"The total charge on an ion is the sum of the oxidation numbers of its atoms.","steps":["X is +6 and there are 4 oxygen atoms at −2 each.","Total = +6 + 4(−2) = +6 − 8. = −2.","So n = 2, and the ion is XO₄²⁻."],"others":"n = 0 would mean +6 − 8 = 0, which is wrong. n = 1 would need X to be +7. n = 3 would need X to be +5.","trap":"Add the charges, then read the sign.","answer":"2"},"chem-11-04-b47":{"idea":"The n-factor is the number of electrons gained per formula unit when the oxidant is reduced.","steps":["KMnO₄: Mn goes +7 to +2, so it gains 5 electrons.","K₂Cr₂O₇: each Cr goes +6 to +3, gaining 3 electrons, and there are 2 Cr atoms, so 6 electrons.","So the pair is 5 and 6."],"others":"1 and 2 are far too low. 7 and 12 use the starting oxidation numbers, not the change. 5 and 3 forget the two Cr atoms.","trap":"Cr₂O₇²⁻ has 2 Cr, so its n-factor is 6.","answer":"5 and 6"},"chem-11-04-b48":{"idea":"When H₂O₂ acts as a reducing agent, its oxygen is oxidised to O₂. Count the electrons lost per H₂O₂.","steps":["In H₂O₂, O is −1.","In O₂, O is 0. Each O atom loses 1 electron.","One H₂O₂ has 2 O atoms.","n-factor = 2 × 1 = 2."],"others":"0 would mean no electron change. 1 counts only one O atom. 4 would count the loss as if the oxygen went from −2.","trap":"Two O atoms per molecule, one electron each.","answer":"2"},"chem-11-04-b49":{"idea":"In a redox reaction, electrons lost must equal electrons gained. The split between oxidant and reductant follows from this.","steps":["As the reductant, one H₂O₂ gives O₂ and loses 2 electrons.","As the oxidant, one H₂O₂ becomes 2 H₂O-type O atoms at −2, gaining 2 electrons.","Both paths move 2 electrons per H₂O₂.","For electrons to balance, 1 mol must be oxidant for every 1 mol that is reductant."],"others":"Equal coefficients in the equation do not force the split. It is the electron balance that does. The O₂ reason is about atoms, not electrons. The 2 versus 1 electron claim is false, since both paths move 2 electrons.","trap":"The electron count decides the ratio.","answer":"Each path moves 2 e- per H2O2 (n-factor 2 both ways), so electrons balance only at 1:1"},"chem-11-04-b50":{"idea":"To name the agents, find which species goes up in oxidation number and which goes down.","steps":["Mn goes from +7 to +2. It gains electrons, so MnO₄⁻ is reduced. It is the oxidising agent.","Fe goes from +2 to +3. It loses electrons, so Fe²⁺ is oxidised. It is the reducing agent.","H⁺ only helps balance the oxygen. It forms H₂O without a change in oxidation number."],"others":"H⁺ does not change oxidation number, so it is neither agent. The pair of agents swapped is wrong. The oxidising agent is reduced, and the species that loses electrons is the reducing agent.","trap":"The oxidising agent gains electrons.","answer":"Oxidizing agent MnO4- (Mn +7 to +2); reducing agent Fe2+ (to Fe3+)"}};
